---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Container from '../../components/common/Container';
import PageHeader from '../../components/common/PageHeader';
import ClosingBanner from '../../components/common/ClosingBanner';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/nav';
import { EXTERNAL_LINK } from '../../components/glyphs';

const title = 'Why AI Enhancer may be unavailable';
const description =
  'Why AI Enhancer may be unavailable on your phone: neural chip requirements, supported processors, Motorola firmware locks and USB DAC setup.';
---

<ContentLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    maxWidth="760px"
    backHref={withBase('/faq')}
    backLabel="Back to FAQ"
    eyebrow="Hardware guide"
    title="Why AI Enhancer may be unavailable"
    description="Processor requirements, sample rate limits, Motorola firmware locks and USB DAC setup."
  />

  {/* Main Centered Editorial Column */}
  <section class="py-12 lg:py-20">
    <Container>
      <article class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] space-y-14 [&_p]:max-w-[68ch]">
        {/* Intro */}
        <div id="overview">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            How AI Enhancer operates
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              AI Enhancer uses a neural network to restore high frequencies. It processes music as it plays. The result is an estimate, not the original master.
            </p>
            <p>
              The feature is off by default. It changes nothing until you turn it on. While active, the badge says Enhanced instead of Bit-perfect.
            </p>
            <p>
              Review these four areas if AI Enhancer is unavailable or turns off.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 1: Processor & neural chip requirements */}
        <div id="processor-requirements" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            1. Processor and neural chip requirements
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            AI Enhancer is designed for a dedicated neural chip. Supported hardware runs the full model. Other phones run Lite mode on the main processor.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Supported neural chips:</strong> Snapdragon 8 Gen 1 through 8 Elite, and Dimensity 8300 through 9400.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Google Tensor and Samsung Exynos:</strong> Supported on recent chips with dedicated models in app updates.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Lite mode on main processor:</strong> Other phones run Lite mode on the main processor.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Real-time processing:</strong> The feature turns off if processing cannot keep up in real time.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">One-time model download:</strong> Each chip requires a one-time model download of 10 to 60 MB.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        {/* Section 2: Sample rate restrictions */}
        <div id="sample-rate-restrictions" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            2. Sample rate and playback restrictions
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            AI Enhancer works only on standard-resolution audio. It turns off automatically when playback conditions do not match:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Standard sample rates only:</strong> Works exclusively on 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz PCM tracks.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Hi-res tracks disabled:</strong> Automatically disabled on 96 kHz, 192 kHz and higher sample rates.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">DSD tracks disabled:</strong> Stays off for DSD audio files and DSD disc images.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Bit-perfect lock active:</strong> Bit-perfect lock turns off all sound processing, including AI Enhancer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Cast playback:</strong> Stays off when streaming audio to cast-enabled speakers and TVs.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">AI Karaoke active:</strong> Disabled while AI Karaoke stem separation is running.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        {/* Section 3: Motorola firmware restrictions */}
        <div id="firmware-restrictions" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            3. Motorola firmware restrictions
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Some device manufacturers lock third-party applications out of the neural chip in their system firmware.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Firmware lock:</strong> Even with a compatible processor, system firmware may block neural chip access.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Motorola devices:</strong> Several Motorola phone models restrict third-party neural access by default.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Automatic fallback:</strong> When the chip is locked, the app falls back to Lite mode.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Official support thread:</strong> You can request neural chip access on Lenovo's official support forum.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <div class="mt-6">
            <a
              href="https://forums.lenovo.com/topic/view/16436/10046553"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded text-small font-semibold text-gold transition-colors duration-[var(--d-ui)] hover:text-gold-light hover:underline focus-visible:focus-ring"
            >
              <span>Ask Lenovo and Motorola for chip access</span>
              <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                {EXTERNAL_LINK.map(({ el: El, ...attrs }) => <El {...attrs}></El>)}
              </svg>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 4: USB DAC setup and troubleshooting */}
        <div id="usb-dac-setup" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            4. USB DAC setup and troubleshooting
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Follow these steps to set up your USB DAC for direct output:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Cable connection:</strong> Connect with a USB-C cable or an OTG adapter. USB-C phones enter host mode automatically.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Android permission:</strong> Connect the DAC and allow access when Android asks. Tick the box to remember this device.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Hardware volume:</strong> Control volume on the DAC hardware. Software volume inside Android alters the bitstream.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Check the signal badge:</strong> The Now Playing badge confirms audio status. It shows Bit-perfect, Enhanced, Resampled or Lossy.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Direct output bypass:</strong> Bitperfect Player talks directly to UAC1 and UAC2 DACs. This skips Android's system mixer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-text-primary">Background playback:</strong> Set battery usage to Unrestricted in Android settings. Lock the app in your recent tasks screen.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <ClosingBanner
    maxWidth="760px"
    eyebrow="Questions?"
    title="Not sure what your phone can do?"
    description="Send your phone model and DAC. We will tell you what works and what does not."
    primaryHref="mailto:support@bit-perfect.com"
    primaryLabel="Email your phone and DAC details"
    secondaryHref={withBase('/faq')}
    secondaryLabel="Read the full FAQ"
  />
</ContentLayout>